# H32 — The Debug build's native stack

Branch: `handover/H32-debug-stack` (from `develop`). Everything is in the working tree; nothing was
committed, branched or pushed.

The ruling: the recursion limit a program meets must be the language's own (design principles 3.9:
1000 open calls), and a native stack that fills first is an accident of the implementation. A
Debug build of the tools now reserves a larger `/STACK`; Release keeps what it had. The numbers
below are measured on Windows x64, this machine (28 logical cores), on a Debug build at `-O0`.

## 1. What changed

`tools/libcamel_tool.cmake`:

```cmake
set(CAMEL_STACK_SIZE_BYTES 33554432)        # every configuration, as before
set(CAMEL_DEBUG_STACK_SIZE_BYTES 134217728) # Debug only
target_link_options(${target} PRIVATE
  "LINKER:/STACK:$<IF:$<CONFIG:Debug>,${CAMEL_DEBUG_STACK_SIZE_BYTES},${CAMEL_STACK_SIZE_BYTES}>")
```

The option is chosen by `$<CONFIG>` in one link option. Two separate `-Xlinker` options do not
work: CMake drops the second as a duplicate and the linker then reads `/STACK:...` as an input
file (this failed once here). Release and RelWithDebInfo keep 32 MiB.

`docs/cli.md`, "Limits", states the Debug value and its derivation next to the existing Windows
stack paragraph.

Verified in the linked PE headers (`llvm-readobj --file-headers`):

| Tree | Binary | `SizeOfStackReserve` | `SizeOfStackCommit` |
|---|---|---|---|
| `out/latest` (Release) | `camel.exe` | 33,554,432 (32 MiB) | 4,096 |
| `out/debug` (Debug) | `camel.exe` | 134,217,728 (128 MiB) | 4,096 |
| `out/latest` | `camel-ls.exe` | 33,554,432 | 4,096 |
| `out/debug` | `camel-ls.exe` | 134,217,728 | 4,096 |

`SizeOfStackCommit` is one page in both trees: the build passes a **reserve only**, and Windows
commits stack pages as they are touched. On Windows the executable's reserve is also the default
of every thread the process creates, and `BigStackThread` takes it when it is larger than 8 MiB
(`include/camel/utils/big_stack_thread.h`), so the `std::tf` / `std::prl` workers get 128 MiB too.

## 2. The frame size of an interpreted call

**Method.** A nested call is refused when less than fifteen sixteenths of the thread's stack is
left (`src/execute/limits.cpp`, Windows: the SEH reserve), i.e. when more than `stack/16` is used.
The diagnostic reports the number of open calls at that moment. Running the same program twice,
on the 32 MiB and on the 128 MiB build, gives the per-call cost as a difference, so the run's own
baseline cancels:

```
frame = (128 MiB - 32 MiB) / 16 / (1605 - 399) = 6144 KiB / 1206 = 5.09 KiB per open call
```

The program (`--max-recursion-depth 1000000`, `std::nvm`, `func depth(n) = n == 0 ? 0 : 1 + depth(n-1)`):

| Stack reserve | Refused at | Diagnostic |
|---|---|---|
| 32 MiB | open call 400 (399 open) | `(399 open calls: the native stack of the thread, 32768 KiB, is full)` |
| 128 MiB | open call 1606 (1605 open) | `(1605 open calls: the native stack of the thread, 131072 KiB, is full)` |

The linear model fits both points exactly (baseline 15.3 KiB: `2048 = 15.3 + 399 x 5.0945`,
`8192 = 15.3 + 1605 x 5.0945` KiB). `test/cases/loops/recursion.cml` (`inside_recursion`) gives
the same 399 on the 32 MiB build, so the function's shape does not change the figure at this
precision.

**This corrects H30's estimate.** H30 estimated "about 90 KiB" by dividing the whole 32 MiB by the
363 calls it reached — it assumed the stack was exhausted. It was not: the Windows guard stops a
call at 1/16 of the stack, so 32 MiB was worth 2 MiB of recursion, and only 399 calls of about
5.1 KiB. The Debug frame is about 5.1 KiB, not 90 KiB; the reason a Debug build failed early is
the 16x the guard holds back, not a 16x frame.

## 3. The language limit

1000 open calls, the same on every scheduler (`kDefaultMaxRecursionDepth`,
`include/camel/execute/limits.h`; design principles 3.9; `docs/cli.md`). Measured on the Debug
`std::nvm`, default limit:

| Program | Result |
|---|---|
| `depth(999)` | prints `999`, exit 0 — 1000 calls open at the bottom, and they fit |
| `depth(1000)` | `Maximum recursion depth (1000) exceeded ...` — the language diagnostic |
| `test/cases/linear/runtime/recursion_depth_limit.cml` | prints `200`, then the same diagnostic at `depth(1200)` |

Before the change the same `inside_recursion` case stopped at 399 open calls with the native-stack
text; after it, the count is reached first on the same binary tree.

## 4. The chosen reserve

The minimum reserve that reaches open call 1001 is

```
16 x (1001 x 5.0945 KiB + 15.3 KiB) = 81,840 KiB = 79.9 MiB
```

because Windows only lets a sixteenth of it be used. **128 MiB** was chosen: 8 MiB usable, which
is 1605 calls, **1.6x the default limit** — a margin of 604 calls, or room for a function whose
per-call native cost is 60% larger than the measured one. It is the next power of two above the
minimum and is not so large that it is worth questioning.

No language limit was changed, and no per-build default depth was introduced.

## 5. What it costs

- **Address space, not memory.** The reserve is 128 MiB per thread; the commit is one page at
  start and grows only as the stack is touched. A recursion at the language limit commits about
  5.0 MiB, and the guard refuses the next call once about 8 MiB is touched, so a deeply recursive
  thread stays at roughly 8 MiB of committed stack. The 120 MiB below that is the SEH reserve the
  guard exists to preserve; it is never touched by ordinary nesting.
- **Per process.** The `std::prl` executor starts one worker per hardware thread (Taskflow's
  default, 28 here), each with the executable's reserve: at most 28 x 128 MiB = 3.5 GiB of
  reserved address space while a parallel pass runs. That is under 0.1% of an x64 process's user
  address space (8 TiB or more), and none of it is committed unless used.
- **32-bit.** A 32-bit build would not fit this (3.5 GiB against a 2 GiB user address space); this
  tree has no 32-bit configuration (the JIT and the arch flags are x86-64 only), so the question
  does not arise today. If one appears, the Debug reserve has to be smaller and a Debug-lower
  default depth would be the honest alternative — but that would change a language limit, which
  is the language author's decision, not this lane's.
- **Release is untouched**: 32 MiB, the same header as before.

