## 7. Open questions

1. **The Windows guard's 15/16 reserve.** It is why the Debug reserve had to be 16x the depth
   cost (and why the old 32 MiB was worth only 2 MiB of recursion). With the reserve at 1/4 the
   Debug `/STACK` could be 20 MiB. The ratio is the SEH decision recorded in
   `src/execute/limits.cpp` and was not touched here; if it is ever revisited, this sizing should
   be redone.
2. **Per-function cost.** 5.09 KiB is for a three-slot function; a function with a much larger
   interpreted frame can still meet the native diagnostic before 1000 on this build. The 1.6x
   margin covers 60% more per call, not every program. There is no per-program check.
3. **A raised limit.** `--max-recursion-depth` above about 1600 in Debug (and above the stack in
   Release) still ends in the native-stack diagnostic. That is the documented fallback and the
   reason the option's range exists; it is not a defect.
4. **The older number.** H30/H31 recorded 363 open calls at 32 MiB; this tree measures 399 for
   the same case. The derivation above uses the differential measured on this tree, so the
   conclusion does not depend on the older figure, but the difference is unexplained.
5. **The tier's timeout budget.** `inside_recursion` now runs in 0.1-0.25 s per back end; the 4x
   Debug timeout multiplier is unchanged and no test parameter was touched.

